const html = "\n<label class=\"field\" style=\"max-width:520px\">Search the questions<input class=\"input\" type=\"search\" data-faq-search></label>\n<section class=\"card\"><h2 class=\"h2\">Where we are today</h2><p class=\"p\">North Kitsap United has a home. In late October 2026, the YMCA of Pierce and Kitsap Counties signed a purchase agreement for approximately 100 acres at Bond Road and Stottlemeyer Road from Raydient Places and Properties, to close in late 2026 or early 2027. At the same time, the Greater Peninsula Conservancy has agreed to acquire the approximately 300 acres next door.</p><p class=\"p\">The community has shown up in a big way. The October 11, 2026 campaign kickoff raised more than $3.5 million, including a $1 million pledge from the Port Gamble S’Klallam Tribe. Campaign giving now totals $8.5 million toward the $25 million goal to build the fields and courts.</p><p class=\"p\">The questions below explain what we’re building, how we got here, and how you can be part of it. We’ll keep this page updated as the project moves forward.</p></section>\n<section class=\"card\"><h2 class=\"h2\">About the project</h2><div><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">What is North Kitsap United (NKU)?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">NKU is a YMCA-centered sports, recreation and well-being campus for all of North Kitsap. It’s a project of the YMCA of Pierce and Kitsap Counties and the Kingston North Kitsap Rotary Club, and will be built on 100 acres at Bond Road and Stottlemeyer Road.</p><p class=\"p\">Think of it as a community third place: somewhere kids, teens, families and seniors come to play, stay healthy and connect, close to home.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">What will be built?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">When fully built, the campus will include:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li>Approximately eight multi-use fields, both turf and grass and some lighted, for soccer, baseball, softball, football, lacrosse and more</li><li>Pickleball, tennis, and basketball courts</li><li>A disc golf course</li><li>A dog park</li><li>An ADA-accessible playground</li><li>A full-scale YMCA</li><li>Access to nearby trails and hiking</li></ul><p class=\"p\">The final layout will be shared on our site plan once design is complete.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Who is doing what?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Each partner brings its own strength:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li><strong>YMCA of Pierce and Kitsap Counties</strong> will own the land and operate the campus. The Y raises funds for and builds its own facility.</li><li><strong>Kingston North Kitsap Rotary Club</strong> leads the $25 million campaign to build the fields and courts.</li><li><strong>Cal Ripken, Sr. Foundation</strong> will build the fields and courts with its general contracting and construction team.</li><li><strong>West Sound Disc Golf Association</strong> will build the disc golf course.</li><li><strong>Greater Peninsula Conservancy</strong> will protect approximately 300 acres next to the campus.</li></ul></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Why does North Kitsap need this?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">North Kitsap has about half the athletic fields expected for a community its size, according to the December 2024 North Kitsap Recreation Facility Feasibility Study by Berk &amp; Associates. More than 80% of existing fields belong to the school district, so public access is limited.</p><p class=\"p\">Youth leagues juggle crowded schedules, late practices, and worn-out fields, and many families drive across the county to play. NKU brings those games, practices, and tournaments home, with room to grow as North Kitsap does.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Why this location?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">It’s central to every North Kitsap community: Kingston, Hansville, Port Gamble, Suquamish, Indianola, and Poulsbo. Rotary spent more than eight years evaluating sites, including many inside the Kingston Urban Growth Area (UGA), and this is the site with the room, the access, and the long-term flexibility the campus needs.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Why a YMCA?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Pairing a Y with public fields creates a true hub for health and connection. The Y brings programs that North Kitsap doesn’t have today, including childcare, teen programs, senior services, and family support, plus financial assistance so everyone can take part.</p><p class=\"p\">More than 3,300 North Kitsap households are already Y members, and many drive to Silverdale. YMCA market research found a central North Kitsap location would serve far more people than one in Poulsbo, which sits too close to the Silverdale Y.</p></div></details></div></section>\n<section class=\"card\"><h2 class=\"h2\">Where we are and where we’re going</h2><div><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">How did we get here?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Rotary first identified the need for more fields in 2016 and announced a youth sports and recreation project at its February 2023 auction. In June 2023, Rotary, the YMCA, and Raydient (the landowner) announced they would explore a campus together.</p><p class=\"p\">The original plan depended on a rezone. When the County Commissioners declined it on June 23, 2025, the partners moved onto a simpler path: buying the land outright, with no housing component.</p><p class=\"p\">Along the way, more than 60 government and nonprofit organizations and 3,750+ households have endorsed the project, and a partnership between Kingston North Kitsap Rotary and NKU was the #1 fundraiser at the March 2026 Kitsap Great Give, raising over $125,000.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">What happened with the rezone?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">The rezone is no longer part of the project. After it was declined in June 2025, the partners shifted to a land purchase, which is set to close at the end of 2026 or beginning of 2027. Housing is not part of NKU, and the campus does not require a zoning change.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">How is fundraising going?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Strongly. Campaign giving totals $8.5 million toward the $25 million needed to build the fields and courts. Highlights include:</p><ul class=\"list\"><li>A $1 million pledge from the Port Gamble S’Klallam Tribe, over five years</li><li>More than $3.5 million raised at the October 11, 2026 kickoff, including pledges from Kingston Kiwanis Club, Kingston North Kitsap Rotary Club and Kingston Youth Sports Association</li><li>A $1 million anonymous endowment from a Kingston resident to help maintain the fields and courts for the long term</li><li>Top fundraiser in Kitsap County at the March 2026 Great Give, together with the Kingston North Kitsap Rotary Club, raising more than $125,000</li></ul><p class=\"p\">The YMCA is raising funds separately for its own facility.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">What’s the timeline?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">With the land secured, the next steps are design, permitting, and construction. We hope to begin construction on the fields and early amenities such as the playground, dog park and disc golf course in late 2027 or early 2028. The YMCA facility will follow in a later phase.</p><p class=\"p\">We’ll post milestones in <a class=\"link\" href=\"/materials?view=news\">Materials / News</a> as each one happens.</p></div></details></div></section>\n<section class=\"card\"><h2 class=\"h2\">The campus and the community</h2><div><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Can the public use the fields without a YMCA membership?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Yes. The fields and courts will be open to the whole community, no membership required. Teams and clubs can reserve fields for a modest fee that goes toward upkeep, and casual play is welcome.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Will the fields be turf or grass?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Both. The plan includes a mix of turf and grass fields, so the campus can support year-round play while balancing cost, maintenance, and environmental care.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Who will maintain the campus?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">The YMCA will own and operate the campus, bringing decades of experience running community facilities. A $1 million anonymous endowment from a Kingston resident will help support ongoing maintenance of the fields and courts.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Will this raise my property taxes?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">No. NKU is funded by private donations, sponsorships, and grants, not local taxes. The YMCA raises funds for its own facility from its members and donors. By adding fields that the public sector would otherwise need to provide, NKU helps reduce future County recreation costs.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">What about traffic, the environment and noise?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">We’ve been planning for all three since the beginning. The initial studies are done, and their findings are shaping the design. Early site studies found highly permeable soils that are good for stormwater, and no streams or standing wetlands were observed. Traffic studies point to improvements near Bond Road, such as signals or roundabouts.</p><p class=\"p\">The project will go through full State Environmental Policy Act (SEPA) review, covering water quality, traffic, noise, and lighting. We’re partnering with Kitsap County and WSDOT so that any needed improvements are built into the broader project plan. And with the Greater Peninsula Conservancy protecting the neighboring 300 acres, forest and wildlife corridors next to the campus are conserved.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Will there be trails and access to Port Gamble Forest Heritage Park?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Yes, that’s part of the vision. The campus will connect to nearby trails and hiking, and we’re working with land stewards and trail groups to keep connections to Port Gamble Forest Heritage Park. We’re also exploring links to the planned ADA-accessible Sound to Olympic Trail, so people can walk or bike in.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">How will people without a car get there?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Kitsap Transit is planning a park-and-ride near the site, served by public transit. Trail connections will add safe walking and biking routes.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Is this consistent with rural zoning and the Growth Management Act?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Yes. Sports and recreation facilities are allowed in the Rural Wooded zone under current Kitsap County code, with no zoning change needed. The campus concentrates facilities in a limited footprint and keeps open space and wildlife corridors, which fits the goals of the Growth Management Act.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Does NKU compete with the Poulsbo Event &amp; Recreation Center (PERC)?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">No, they work together. PERC’s two tournament-size soccer fields, a partnership with Olympic College, are a welcome addition. North Kitsap’s need is large enough that both projects are needed, and we’re coordinating with the City of Poulsbo so facilities complement each other.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">What about the fields we have now?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">NKU adds to existing fields rather than replacing them, and it relieves the pressure that wears them out. Rotary has a long record of supporting local facilities, including Kingston Middle School field improvements with the school district and KYSA, and lights at Kingston High School. Local nonprofits and schools can apply for Rotary club grants.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">How will NKU benefit the local economy?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Regional games, tournaments, and camps will bring visitors to local shops, restaurants, and lodging. Early research by the Kitsap Economic Development Alliance estimates the project could bring more than $100 million in economic benefit to Kitsap County.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Does Rotary profit from this project?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">No. Kingston North Kitsap Rotary is a nonprofit service club. Its role is to bring people and resources together, as it did for the Village Green Community Center, while the YMCA owns and runs the campus.</p></div></details></div></section>\n<section class=\"card\"><h2 class=\"h2\">About giving</h2><div><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Is my gift tax-deductible?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Gifts are made to the Kingston North Kitsap Rotary Foundation, a 501(c)(3) nonprofit (EIN 20-2960627), and are tax-deductible to the extent allowed by law. You’ll receive a receipt for your records. Please consult your tax advisor.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Who receives my gift, and how is it used?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Cash gifts go to the Kingston North Kitsap Rotary Foundation, designated for NKU. They fund the fields and courts on the campus. In-kind gifts of work, materials, or equipment are reviewed by the YMCA and the Cal Ripken, Sr. Foundation, and the YMCA acknowledges accepted gifts.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">What are the ways to give?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">You can give by check, credit card, wire transfer, appreciated stock, a donor-advised fund grant, or an IRA qualified charitable distribution if you’re 70½ or older. Our <a class=\"link\" href=\"/donate\">Donate page</a> has instructions for each. If you give by check, wire, stock, DAF or IRA, please fill out the short “Tell us about your gift” form so we can watch for it and thank you.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Can I pledge over several years?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Yes. Many of our donors spread their gifts over up to five years. Choose your total, the number of installments, and whether to pay annually or semi-annually, and we’ll confirm the schedule with you in writing.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">How will I be recognized? Can I give anonymously?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">You choose. Gifts are recognized at nine levels, from Rising Star Sponsor ($1,000+) to Legacy Partner ($1,000,000). Donors who opt in are listed by the name they choose, never with amounts. If you’d prefer, just check “keep my gift anonymous.”</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">Can my business help?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">Yes, in several ways: sponsor at one of the nine levels, explore naming opportunities for fields, plazas, and other amenities, or run a “Top Off for NKU” round-up at your register.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">How do I offer construction help or materials?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">We welcome offers of site work, materials, and equipment. Use the <a class=\"link\" href=\"/donate/in-kind\">In-Kind offer form</a> on our Donate page. The YMCA and the Cal Ripken, Sr. Foundation review each offer. For safety, volunteers do not work on active construction.</p></div></details><details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qa-q\">How else can I help?</summary><div class=\"qa-a\"><p class=\"p\">You don’t need to give money to make a difference. Share our story, introduce us to donors or businesses, host a gathering, write a letter of support, help find grants, or volunteer at events. Visit <a class=\"link\" href=\"/get-involved\">Get Involved</a> to tell us how you’d like to help, and sign up for our newsletter to follow the progress.</p></div></details></div></section>\n<div class=\"btn-row\"><a class=\"btn btn-gold\" href=\"/donate\">Donate</a><a class=\"btn btn-ghost\" href=\"/get-involved\">Get Involved</a></div>\n";
---
